---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../lib/site.mjs";
---

<Base
  title="The Book"
  description="Overview of The Ministry of Doubt — conspiracy, power and how to weigh evidence when authorities and critics can both be wrong."
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
    <header class="hero" style="border-bottom: none; margin-bottom: 0; padding-bottom: 0.5rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The book</p>
      <h1>{SITE.name}</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Working subtitle: <em>Conspiracy, Power and the Battle Over What We Are Allowed to Know</em>
      </p>
    </header>

    <p class="principle">{SITE.tagline}</p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      A nonfiction investigation of the difficult territory between automatic trust in official
      narratives and indiscriminate belief in conspiracy theories. It is not a museum of secret
      truths, and it is not a defence of institutions as such.
    </p>
    <p>
      The book develops a portable method for assigning confidence when the people who hold the
      records are also parties to the story — then stress-tests that method on documented deceptions,
      ordinary institutional failure, contested narratives and systems of money, attention and soft power.
    </p>

    <h2>How it thinks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>When the model and reality disagree, reality wins.</li>
      <li>Garbage in, garbage out.</li>
      <li>Missing evidence stays missing — it is not a licence to invent the preferred ending.</li>
      <li>One demonstrated lie changes credibility; it does not prove every related claim false.</li>
      <li>Institutions that control evidence carry a greater burden of transparency.</li>
      <li>A real crisis can be exploited without having been deliberately created.</li>
      <li>Coordinated-looking outcomes often come from incentives, bureaucracy, fear and groupthink — not only from a central plot.</li>
      <li>The hard question: <strong>What evidence would make you change your mind?</strong></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Shape of the argument</h2>
    <p>Public overview only — not a chapter dump.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Method first</h3>
        <p>Categories of explanation, evidence hierarchy, commissions, leaks and falsifiability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Documented cases</h3>
        <p>Where conspiracy or institutional deception is already on the historical record.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Failure without cabals</h3>
        <p>Capture, incentives and error that look coordinated without a secret centre.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Contested narratives</h3>
        <p>Few deep stress tests — steelman both sides, separate process failure from object-level claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Power systems</h3>
        <p>Money, attention markets, surveillance and soft elite coordination — without demonology.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Staying sane</h3>
        <p>Language, psychology of belief, negative controls and a personal protocol for doubt without paranoia.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Companion media</h2>
    <p>
      Planned alongside the manuscript: this site, a public <a href="/tracker">evidence tracker</a>
      (electiontracker-style ledger of sources and scores), and later album and documentary concepts.
      The tracker is intended to launch with the book — not as a substitute for reading carefully.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      Status: architecture and outline in private development. No sample chapters are published here yet.
      For press or rights enquiries, use the <a href="/about">contact address</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/author">Meet the author</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/tracker">Tracker plans</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
